data.content:
## What Changed

.NET 10's `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` upgraded from **Microsoft.OpenApi v1** to **v2**. This is a major breaking change across the object model. Here's each issue and its fix:

### 1. `OpenApiString` / `OpenApiAny` types removed
OpenApi v2 uses `System.Text.Json.Nodes.JsonNode` instead of `IOpenApiAny`:

```csharp
// Before (.NET 9)
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Any;
param.Schema.Example = new OpenApiString("1.0");

// After (.NET 10)
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
param.Schema.Example = JsonValue.Create("1.0");
```

### 2. `OpenApiResponse` construction changed
Responses now use `Description` as a required constructor parameter:

```csharp
// Before
operation.Responses.TryAdd("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });

// After
operation.Responses.Add("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });
```
> `Responses` is now `Dictionary<string, OpenApiResponse>` — `TryAdd` still works but verify the key type hasn't changed to an enum/status code in your version.

### 3. `OpenApiReference` and `ReferenceType` restructured
`ReferenceType` moved and `OpenApiReference` constructor changed:

```csharp
// Before
new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference { Type = ReferenceType.SecurityScheme, Id = "oauth2" }
};

// After
new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference("oauth2", ReferenceType.SecurityScheme)
};
```

### 4. `OpenApiSecurityRequirement` is no longer a dictionary
It changed from `Dictionary<OpenApiSecurityScheme, IList<string>>` to having a `Schemes` property:

```csharp
// Before
operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
{
    new() { [oAuthScheme] = scopes }
};

// After
operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
{
    new()
    {
        Schemes = new Dictionary<OpenApiSecurityScheme, IList<string>>
        {
            [oAuthScheme] = scopes
        }
    }
};
```

### 5. `Nullable` property removed from schema
OpenApi v2 uses the `Type` flags enum (`JsonSchemaType`) instead of a `bool Nullable`:

```csharp
// Before
property.Value.Nullable = false;

// After — remove Null flag from Type
property.Value.Type &= ~JsonSchemaType.Null;

// To make nullable:
// property.Value.Type |= JsonSchemaType.Null;
```

Add this using:
```csharp
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models;  // JsonSchemaType is here
```

### Full corrected code

```csharp
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models;

options.AddOperationTransformer((operation, context, ct) =>
{
    var param = operation.Parameters.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Name == "api-version");
    if (param is not null)
    {
        param.Schema.Example = JsonValue.Create("1.0");
    }

    operation.Responses.TryAdd("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });

    var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecurityScheme
    {
        Reference = new OpenApiReference("oauth2", ReferenceType.SecurityScheme)
    };
    operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
    {
        new()
        {
            Schemes = new Dictionary<OpenApiSecurityScheme, IList<string>>
            {
                [oAuthScheme] = scopes
            }
        }
    };

    return Task.CompletedTask;
});

options.AddSchemaTransformer((schema, context, ct) =>
{
    if (schema.Properties is not null)
    {
        foreach (var property in schema.Properties)
        {
            if (schema.Required?.Contains(property.Key) != true)
                property.Value.Type &= ~JsonSchemaType.Null;
        }
    }
    return Task.CompletedTask;
});
```

**Summary:** Remove `Microsoft.OpenApi.Any` usages → use `JsonNode`; update `OpenApiReference` to constructor syntax; use `JsonSchemaType` flags instead of `Nullable`; update `OpenApiSecurityRequirement` to use `Schemes`.
